{"id":"hessian-matrix-and-convexity-of-linear-regressio","t":"Hessian and convexity of the ridge loss","tags":["Optimization","Regression"],"d":"Medium","rt":"numpy","fn":"ridge_hessian","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"For linear regression with an L2 penalty,\n\n$$L(w) = \\frac{1}{2n}\\lVert Xw - y\\rVert_2^2 + \\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\lVert w\\rVert_2^2$$\n\nthe Hessian $\\nabla^2 L$ decides everything about the landscape: whether there's a unique minimum, and how fast gradient descent can get there.\n\nWrite `ridge_hessian(X, lam)` that returns a tuple `(H, eig_min, eig_max, strictly_convex)`:\n\n- `H`: the Hessian matrix, shape `(d, d)`.\n- `eig_min`, `eig_max`: its smallest and largest eigenvalues, as Python floats.\n- `strictly_convex`: Python `bool`, `True` when `eig_min > 1e-10 * max(1.0, eig_max)`.\n\n## Inputs\n- `X`: float array `(n, d)` with $n \\ge 1$; `lam` $\\ge 0$.\n- The function doesn't take `y` or `w`. Work out why it doesn't need them.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef ridge_hessian(X, lam):\n    \"\"\"Return (Hessian of the ridge loss, smallest eigenvalue, largest eigenvalue, strictly convex?).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Differentiate twice. The gradient is $\\frac1n X^\\top(Xw - y) + \\lambda w$. What is left after differentiating once more with respect to $w$?","$H = \\frac1n X^\\top X + \\lambda I$. It doesn't depend on $w$ or $y$, because the loss is a quadratic in $w$.","Use `np.linalg.eigvalsh` (for symmetric matrices, ascending order). $X^\\top X$ is positive semidefinite, so `eig_min` can only be 0 (up to rounding) when $X$ has dependent columns and $\\lambda = 0$."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef ridge_hessian(X, lam):\n    X = np.asarray(X, dtype=float)\n    n, d = X.shape\n    H = X.T @ X / n + lam * np.eye(d)            # constant: the loss is exactly quadratic in w\n    eig = np.linalg.eigvalsh(H)                  # symmetric: real eigenvalues, ascending\n    lo, hi = float(eig[0]), float(eig[-1])\n    return H, lo, hi, bool(lo > 1e-10 * max(1.0, hi))\n","ex_md":"## Why the Hessian is constant\n$L$ is a quadratic in $w$: $\\frac{1}{2}w^\\top H w - \\frac1n y^\\top X w + \\text{const}$. The second derivative of a quadratic is its matrix, so $H = \\frac1n X^\\top X + \\lambda I$ everywhere. Neither $y$ nor $w$ appears.\n\n## Convexity from eigenvalues\n$v^\\top X^\\top X v = \\lVert Xv\\rVert^2 \\ge 0$, so $X^\\top X$ is always positive semidefinite and the loss is always convex. It's **strictly** convex (a unique minimiser) iff $H \\succ 0$, i.e. $\\lambda > 0$ or $X$ has full column rank. Duplicate or collinear features (tests 2 and 4), or fewer samples than features (test 6), give a flat valley of equally good solutions; any $\\lambda > 0$ lifts every eigenvalue by $\\lambda$ and removes it. That's ridge's original motivation.\n\n## Why both extreme eigenvalues matter\n$\\lambda_{\\max}$ caps the gradient-descent step size ($\\eta < 2/\\lambda_{\\max}$), and $\\kappa = \\lambda_{\\max}/\\lambda_{\\min}$ sets the convergence speed. Test 7 scales features by up to 100x, which makes $\\kappa$ about $10^4$: that's why you standardise features before gradient descent.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(nd^2)$ to form $H$, $O(d^3)$ for the eigenvalues.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Using `np.linalg.eig`: it may return complex numbers with tiny imaginary parts and in no particular order.\n- Testing `eig_min > 0` exactly. Rounding turns a true 0 into $\\pm10^{-16}$, so a relative threshold is needed.\n- Forgetting the $1/n$, which scales every eigenvalue by $n$ but leaves the convexity verdict unchanged.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Logistic regression's Hessian is $\\frac1n X^\\top \\mathrm{diag}(p(1-p)) X$. Is the loss strictly convex on separable data? What happens to the weights?\n- How does batch normalisation relate to conditioning the Hessian of the next layer?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[1., 0.],\n       [0., 2.]])\n0.0","out":"(array([[0.5, 0. ],\n       [0. , 2. ]]), 0.5, 2.0, True)"},{"in":"array([[1., 1.],\n       [2., 2.]])\n0.0","out":"(array([[2.5, 2.5],\n       [2.5, 2.5]]), 0.0, 5.0, False)"}],"lessons":[{"href":"../ml-maths-course/index.html#l07-01","t":"Quadratic Forms","c":"ML Maths"},{"href":"../ml-maths-course/index.html#l08-03","t":"Linear Regression With Matrices","c":"ML Maths"},{"href":"../machine-learning/index.html#l02-02","t":"Ridge, lasso and elastic net","c":"Machine Learning"}],"related":["gradient-of-multivariate-linear-regression-loss","learning-rate-stability-and-convergence-bounds","pca-projection"],"ntests":8,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 2.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[0.5, 0.0], [0.0, 2.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.5, 2.0, true]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 1.0], [2.0, 2.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[2.5, 2.5], [2.5, 2.5]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.0, 5.0, false]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 1.0], [2.0, 2.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.5], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[3.0, 2.5], [2.5, 3.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.5, 5.5, true]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[-0.5947236978403764, 0.630783487419836, -1.8562906726800485], [1.0393541232237575, 1.0309217445385483, -1.0224893658533392], [1.8178460987289433, -0.3851893808502385, 2.5882248604294205], [0.5441771887526803, -0.36622168185723664, 1.2766205524671537], [-1.4248485130063353, -0.7038591205388717, -0.017130271928591956], [0.1361623384532617, -0.9151747291399203, 1.9665117967331023], [-0.19147101971894068, 1.1202500757479104, -2.4319711712147614], [0.5704516907585605, 0.5723436551805909, -0.5742356196026214], [0.34512242791942843, -0.1752757919726442, 0.6956740118647169], [-1.867742907984139, 0.9914585596358675, -3.850660027255874]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.105302933254695, -0.11251556495109713, 1.3303340631568896], [-0.11251556495109713, 0.5672388247733692, -1.2469932144978357], [1.330334063156